\begin_layout Standard
This chapter develops tailored spectral methods for several important applications arising in statistical data science and machine learning, where the quantities of interest can be extracted by computing the top eigenspace and eigenvalues of a properly constructed data matrix. Encouragingly, we shall see that these methods are all variations on a common recipe. The classical 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell_2$
\end_inset

 perturbation theory introduced in Chapter
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "cha:matrix-perturbation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, when equipped with statistical tools reviewed in Section
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:matrix-Bernstein"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, unveils appealing performance of spectral methods in a variety of applications by harnessing the randomness of data.
\end_layout

