\begin_layout Section
Matrix denoising
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:matrix-denoising-L2"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us start by trying out the approach we have introduced on a simple model: matrix denoising.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Problem formulation and algorithm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider an unknown rank-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 symmetric matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star} \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 with eigen-decomposition 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}=\bm{U}^{\star}\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}\bm{U}^{\star\top}$
\end_inset

, where the columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}^{\star}\in \mathbb{R}^{n\times r}$
\end_inset

 are orthonormal, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}\in \mathbb{R}^{r\times r}$
\end_inset

 is a diagonal matrix containing the nonzero eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

. Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $|\lambda_1^{\star}|\geq |\lambda_2^{\star}|\geq \cdots \geq |\lambda_r^{\star}|>0$
\end_inset

. Suppose that we observe a noisy copy 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}=\bm{M}^{\star}+\bm{E}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{E}=[E_{i,j}]_{1\leq i,j\leq n}$
\end_inset

 is a symmetric noise matrix. It is assumed that the entries 
\begin_inset Formula $\{{E}_{i,j}\}_{i\geq j}$
\end_inset

 are independently generated obeying 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
	E_{i,j} \overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2) , \qquad i\geq j. 
\end{align}
\end_inset

The aim is to estimate the eigenspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 from the data matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}$
\end_inset

. This is a simple setting that has been frequently studied in the literature, and is closely related to the angular/phase synchronization problem 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
citep{singer2011angular,abbe2020entrywise}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to estimate the low-rank factors specified by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}^{\star}$
\end_inset

, a natural scheme is to resort to the rank-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 leading eigenspace of the data matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}$
\end_inset

. More precisely, denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_1,\cdots,\lambda_n$
\end_inset

 the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}$
\end_inset

 sorted by their magnitudes, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
	|\lambda_1| \geq |\lambda_2| \geq \cdots \geq |\lambda_n|, 
\end{align}
\end_inset

and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{u}_1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\cdots$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{u}_n$
\end_inset

 represent the associated eigenvectors. This spectral method returns 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U} = [\bm{u}_1,\cdots,\bm{u}_r]\in \mathbb{R}^{n\times r}$
\end_inset

 as the estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}^{\star}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Performance guarantees
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now examine the accuracy of the above spectral estimate. Towards this, a key step lies in bounding the spectral norm of the noise matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{E}=[E_{i,j}]_{1\leq i,j\leq n}$
\end_inset

. We claim that with probability at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1-O(n^{-8})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
	\|\bm{E}\| \leq 5\sigma\sqrt{n}.
	\label{eq:noise-matrix-E-bound-denoising}
\end{align}
\end_inset

Armed with this bound and the fact 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{r+1}^{\star}=0$
\end_inset

, the Davis-Kahan theorem (cf.
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

Corollary 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "cor:davis-kahan-conclusion-corollary"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) reveals that with probability greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $1-O(n^{-8})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
	\mathsf{dist}\big(\bm{U},\bm{U}^{\star}\big)\leq\frac{2\|\bm{E}\|}{|\lambda_{r}^{\star}|}\leq\frac{10\sigma\sqrt{n}}{|\lambda_{r}^{\star}|} ,
	\label{eq:dist-U-Ustar-denoising-L2}
\end{align}
\end_inset

provided that the noise variance is sufficiently small 
\begin_inset Formula $ \sigma \sqrt{n} \leq \frac{1-1/\sqrt{2}}{5} |\lambda_r^{\star}|$
\end_inset

 (so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{E}\|\leq (1-1/\sqrt{2})|\lambda_r^{\star}|$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

