\begin_layout Standard
We resort to the spectral method to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
	\label{eq:ground_truth_factors}
	\bL_{\star }:=\bm{U}_{\star}\bm{\Sigma}_{\star}^{1/2}\quad \text{and} \quad  \bR_{\star} :=\bm{V}_{\star}\bm{\Sigma}_{\star}^{1/2},
\end{equation}
\end_inset

which are denoted as the true low-rank factors, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}_{\star}=\bm{U}_{\star}\bm{\Sigma}_{\star}\bm{V}_{\star}^\top$
\end_inset

 stands for its singular value decomposition. Let us construct a 
\emph on
surrogate matrix
\emph default
 as follows 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
	\label{eq:surrogate}
	\bm{Y} = \frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^m y_i \bm{A}_i = \mathcal{A}^* \mathcal{A} (\bm{M}_{\star}),
\end{equation}
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}:\mathbb{R}^{n_1\times n_2}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 is the linear operator defined by: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
	\mathcal{A}(\bm{X}):=\big[m^{-1/2}\langle\bm{A}_{i},\bm{X}\rangle\big]_{1\leq i\leq m},\label{eq:defn-A-sensing}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}^\ast$
\end_inset

 denotes its adjoint. The generic version of the spectral method then proceeds by computing (i) two matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}\in \mathbb{R}^{n_1\times r}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}\in \mathbb{R}^{n_2\times r}$
\end_inset

 whose columns consist of the top-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 left and right singular vectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{Y}$
\end_inset

, respectively, and (ii) a diagonal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}\in \mathbb{R}^{r\times r}$
\end_inset

 that contains the corresponding top-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 singular values. In the hope that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}$
\end_inset

 are reasonably reliable estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{\star}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}_{\star}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}_{\star}$
\end_inset

, respectively, we take 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align} \label{eq:initial_guess}
	\bm{L}_0 = \bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}^{1/2} \qquad \text{and} \qquad \bm{R}_0 = \bm{V}\bm{\Sigma}^{1/2}
\end{align}
\end_inset

as estimates of the low-rank factors 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{L}_{\star}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{R}_{\star}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:ground_truth_factors"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}_{\star}\in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 is known to be positive semidefinite, then we can also let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}\in \mathbb{R}^{n\times r}$
\end_inset

 be a matrix consisting of the top-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 leading eigenvectors, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}$
\end_inset

 being a diagonal matrix containing all top-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 eigenvalues.
\end_layout

