\begin_layout Standard
The blind deconvolution problem has a similar mathematical structure to that of phase retrieval. Recall the sensing model in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:samples-BD"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

. Instead of reconstructing a symmetric rank-1 matrix, we now aim to recover an 
\emph on
asymmetric
\emph default
 rank-1 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\vhp \vxp^\mathsf{H}$
\end_inset

 with sensing matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $\mA_i = \vb_i \va_i^\mathsf{H}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(1 \le i \le m)$
\end_inset

. Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:surrogate"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, we form a surrogate matrix 
\begin_inset Formula \[
	\bm{Y} = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^m y_i \vb_i \va_i^\mathsf{H}.
\]
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{u}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{v}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 denote the leading left singular vector, right singular vector, and singular value, respectively. The initial guess is then formed as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
	\label{eq:init-BD}
	\bm{h}_0 = \sqrt{\sigma} \, \bm{u} \qquad \text{and} \qquad \bm{x}_0 = \sqrt{\sigma} \, \bm{v}.
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This estimate provably reveals sufficient information about the truth, provided that the sample size 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 is sufficiently large. 
